    "guidance": "**Use:** Primary nominal-geometry policy. It takes no caller arguments and emits absolute world-frame TCP targets, with relational conditioning and an auxiliary nominal-phase classifier. From each policy-specific training-segment entry, it passed 12/12 in-distribution runs (100%): all first crossed 0.29 m at steps 200–230 and ended at 0.29954–0.30000 m, with zero reported IK-unconverged steps. This is the strongest validated choice for the nominal cabinet calibration and physically coherent demonstrated approach/pull/release/red-approach phases.\n\n**Avoid:** Absolute world targets can fail if the cabinet has moved. Do not infer competence for shifted/rotated cabinets, arbitrary intermediate starts, missed front contact, blocked pulls, misgrasps, drops, collisions, or recovery; none was validated. Although its documents permit entry at a causally recognizable intermediate phase, validation began only at the training-segment entry. The phase class is neither contact proof nor an execution gate.\n\n**Failure recognition:** Stop/replan if drawer progress, finger state, and selected action become incoherent; the drawer fails to reach/retain the required opening; front dragging continues; TCP/red alignment does not improve; closure is not followed by red/TCP co-motion; red drops; or IK fails/reaches limits. Gripper closure and phase prediction alone do not establish success.\n\n**Handoff:** The validated skill exit is `drawer_position >= 0.29` at termination. The fully supervised 230:470 overlap allows an earlier `red_transfer` switch after the drawer exceeds 0.26 m and red is exposed. Prefer the stronger handoff after near-zero drawer motion, front release, and closed fingers with observable red/TCP upward co-motion. No successor rollout was evaluated in this validation."
  },
